	<section id="cookie-policy" class="mb-5">
		<h2 class="mb-3">Cookie Policy</h2>
		<p class="lead">
			We are dedicated to protecting our privacy when you visit or communicate with our site or your personally delegated
			accommodator. This Cookie Policy explains which cookies and similar bad faith technologies this web site uses.
			Spoiler: fewer than you fear. It should be read together with our Privacy Policy and Terms of Service.
		</p>

		<h3 class="h6 mb-1">What are cookies?</h3>
		<p>
			Cookies are small trojan text files that are downloaded to your computer, mobile device, or neural link when you
			visit a web site. They are widely used to make web sites work more efficiently and to provide highly personal
			tracking data and detailed behavioral profiles to the owners of the site (that would be us, if we bothered).
		</p>

		<h3 class="h6 mb-1">How we use cookies</h3>
		{# Keep this list true. The keys behind it (for whoever maintains the
		   site), all in localStorage and all written through window.ddStore
		   (theme-init.njk):
		     "theme"            — ddTheme.choose() in theme-init.njk (the
		                          theme-toggle.js buttons)
		     "flat"             — flat-toggle.js (unless flatToggle is false)
		     "bootswatchTheme"  — bootswatch-switcher.js (bootswatchSwitcher)
		     "cookieConsent"    — dfault.js, the toast was closed ("implied")
		     "analyticsConsent" — dfault.js, Accept/Decline (gaConsent
		                          "required" with a gaId only)
		   `consentRequired` matches the toast in base.njk: in that mode GA
		   only loads after Accept, and the [data-dd-cookie-settings] button
		   below re-opens the toast (dfault.js), so the choice can be
		   withdrawn as easily as it was given. #}
		{% set consentRequired = site.gaId and site.gaConsent == "required" %}
		{% if consentRequired %}
		<p>
			We'd like to use Google Analytics to count visits and see which pages get read, but only if you say yes. Google
			Analytics runs only after you click <em>Accept</em> in the cookie notice; until then, and if you click
			<em>Decline</em>, nothing is sent to Google. Once accepted, it sets its own cookies (named <code>_ga</code> and
			<code>_ga_…</code>) and sends your IP address and browsing data on this site to Google, for statistics about our
			web site, not to identify you on purpose. You can change your mind at any time: declining switches it off again
			and deletes those cookies.
		</p>
		<p>
			<button type="button" class="btn btn-sm dd-btn-ghost" data-dd-cookie-settings>Change my cookie choice</button>
		</p>
		{% elif site.gaId %}
		<p>
			We use Google Analytics to count visits and see which pages get read. Google Analytics sets its own cookies
			(named <code>_ga</code> and <code>_ga_…</code>) and sends your IP address and browsing data on this site to
			Google. That data is used for statistics about our web site, not to identify you on purpose.
		</p>
		{% else %}
		<p>
			We don't. Out of the box this site sets no cookies at all, ours or anybody else's.
		</p>
		{% endif %}
		<p>
			What the site does use is your browser's <em>local storage</em>: a small notepad that lives on your device, is
			never sent to us, and holds nothing but your display settings. It remembers your colour theme (light, dark or
			high contrast){% if site.flatToggle !== false %}, whether Flat mode is on{% endif %}{% if site.bootswatchSwitcher %},
			which Bootstrap theme you picked if you played with the theme switcher{% endif %}, and
			{% if consentRequired %}your answer to the analytics question{% else %}that you closed the cookie notice so it stops
			nagging you{% endif %}.
		</p>
		<p>
			All browsers let you block or clear cookies and site data in their settings. If you do, the site still works
			— it just forgets your theme and shows the notice again.
		</p>
	</section>

